How to fit tests
  We have unknown pools of students taking the 3 tests (xuece, tongce, zhikao)
  We fit a piecewise linear function (or step function if that's easier) to the pdfs of these three functions
  Integrals must match the observed number of students
  Probably we only want 2-3 steps or 2 linear pieces for each test. Optimize to reduce error in the agreement between thresholds where they overlap.
  Test outcomes are percentiles in the test taker pool. All we want is to convert these to percentiles in the original pool. There is no need for any other fancy distortion or correlation.

I determined that 2 steps = 3 parameters, 2 lines = 5 parameters, 3 steps = 5 parameters, etc. If it's not too expensive we could fit more steps/lines or even higher order polynomials. But we should probably require the curves to me unimodal. And using too many parameters leads to overfitting so 3 steps is  fine for now.

There are 400-600 senior high schools. Is it realistic for us to get the matrix of which high schools are sending students to which universities? Or perhaps get this data for just a few top high schools or top universities?


Consider the following model: all measurements have some underlying correlation matrix. E.g. ranks in class, xuece, tongce, and zhikao. Simultaneous thresholds on rank + xuece gives us a distribution over underlying ability. Xuece (low) + zhikao (high) give us another. One threshold on xuece, or tongce give a different distribution. The school will likely try to make the median or mean match.

  - 學測個申: 65,453 passed stage 1; 44,025 were finally allocated — about 67%.
  - 統測甄選: 36,800 passed stage 1; 27,839 received at least one 正/備取 qualification — about 76%; 24,421 were finally allocated — about 66%.

The apparent contradiction is that a department normally screens about 3× its quota, while each student applies to several departments. A candidate can pass some screens even if they fail others.

24 matched 第八類 department-years.
  - 562 students passed the published screen.
  - Those represent 314 final seats: a 1.79× screen.

The interviews for 統測 and 學測 are somewhere between 1.5x and 3x filters. Although a student might still end up at the school they want, they might get pushed to a worse department. I don't have a good way to handle this.

Sounds great in theory but is it realistic to implement? Will the runtime be reasonable? Will it converge? Do we have enough datapoints to avoid overfitting?

繁星 rank is within-school. The published cutoff is one national class-rank bar applied inside every nominating school, so the admitted set is a mixture over school means μ_s. That's a second
  quadrature
    dimension and one variance parameter. Nothing in the 繁星 cutoffs identifies the spread of μ_s — but high-school-destinations.tsv does, which is the natural use for that table. ---- I'm hesitant to
  add complexity here. Do it without this at first and explain in a note somewhere how we would tack it on. ----- Screen ≠ admit. For 個申 and 甄選 the threshold is a 1.5–3× screen with review after, so
  you need E[A | passed screen, top by review], with review as one more loaded measurement. That's your 24 matched
    第八類 department-years earning their keep. ---- Same here, just treat these as thresholds for now, add to the note above about how to correct it. ---- A free spline density and a loading are
  near-degenerate: both compress implied levels toward the middle, and only the specific inverse-Mills shape separates them. Pin participation first —
    pool/complement.py's cohort-share constraint is exactly the right kind — and λ becomes identifiable. --- ok so pin a few parameters to make this correct?
